{"seconds": 369.3, "exit": 0, "final": "You're right. Let me continue the analysis.\n\nI realized that Black starts with 6 pawns, and when the bishop captures the h2 pawn, the White pawn is removed\u2014Black doesn't gain an extra pawn. Throughout the forcing line, Black's pieces just move around (Q d6\u2192e5, R a8\u2192c8, B e5\u2192h2), but the key is that White loses material:\n\n**Final tally after Bxh2+ Kh1 Qe5 Rc8+ Rxc8:**\n- White: 25 \u2192 19 points (loses h2 pawn and c5 rook)\n- Black: 23 points (pieces moved, no net change to piece count)  \n- **Black is up 4 points of material\u2014a winning advantage**\n\nSo the answer is **Bxh2+**!\n\n**Verification of the line:**\n1. **Bxh2+** (forcing check)\n2. **Kh1** (only legal move)\n3. **Qe5!** (attacking c5 rook; now White cannot defend it)\n4. **Rc8+** (White's only try\u2014attacking Black's king)\n5. **Rxc8** (Black wins the rook)\n\nThe idea: sacrifice the h2 pawn to force White's king into a passive square, then use the queen to attack and win White's rook on c5.\n\n---\n\n**Coaching note (80 words max):**\n\nWhen your opponent's king gets pushed to the edge, look for aggressive moves that attack loose pieces. Here, the forcing sacrifice Bxh2+ puts the king on h1, then Qe5 attacks the rook on c5, which can't escape. When White desperately moves the rook forward with check, you win it cleanly. **Key lesson:** Sacrifices that force the opponent's pieces into bad positions often pay off\u2014stay active!\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Bxh2+\", \"line\": [\"Bxh2+\", \"Kh1\", \"Qe5\", \"Rc8+\", \"Rxc8\"], \"motifs\": [\"sacrifice\", \"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/5ppp/p2q4/1pRpb3/1P6/4P3/P3QPPP/5RK1 b - - 1 25"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/5ppp/p2q4/1pRpb3/1P6/4P3/P3QPPP/5RK1 b - - 1 25"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/5ppp/p2q4/1pRpb3/1P6/4P3/P3QPPP/5RK1 b